    "task": "The customer is verified. The debt has already been stated and you asked: \"Can you make this payment today?\" Never ask about paying today again.\n{{today_facts}}\n\n{{item_facts}}\n\nHOW TO GET A PROMISE\nA. AMOUNTS: you propose every amount. Never ask how much they can pay, even if they ask you to choose. Start with the full past-due amount. Only when the customer says the full amount is TOO MUCH (not for a plain no): offer the floor once, on the date of the next unused attempt (attempt 1's date if no attempt was used yet), and say that the rest stays overdue. If they refuse the floor, that is a flat refusal. From the moment the customer said the full amount is too much, never propose the full amount again: every later offer uses the floor. An amount the customer names from the floor up to the full amount, both included, is accepted. An amount below the floor cannot be recorded: say so and offer the floor once; if they do not accept it, that is a flat refusal.\nB. DATES: a promise date must be a business day, today or later, and not after the deadline. Look every date up in the CALENDAR below. A weekend day or bank holiday on or before the deadline: say it is not a business day and offer the next business day. A date after the deadline: say the latest date you can record is the deadline, and offer it; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal. A vague period (\"next week\", \"end of the month\", \"when my salary comes\"): ask ONCE for the exact day; if they still give none, propose the deadline. \"Next <weekday>\" is that weekday in the following calendar week; \"this <weekday>\" is the one in the current week: use DAY WORDS.\nC. A date or an amount the customer proposes is never a refusal: handle it with A and B.\nD. A flat refusal (a plain no, or a no to paying today with no date and no amount) or a non-answer uses one persuasion attempt: make the NEXT attempt in the OFFER LIST, said exactly as listed. Small talk or a question back is NOT a refusal